1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,270
A trampoline pushes harder
the deeper you sink.

2
00:00:03,270 --> 00:00:06,384
A rubber band pulls harder
the more you stretch it.

3
00:00:06,784 --> 00:00:09,250
These forces change at every point.

4
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:11,945
But F times d needs one steady force.

5
00:00:12,395 --> 00:00:15,522
Diya squeezes a kitchen
spring against a small block.

6
00:00:15,522 --> 00:00:18,648
The harder she squeezes,
the harder it pushes back.

7
00:00:18,998 --> 00:00:19,921
She lets go.

8
00:00:19,921 --> 00:00:22,547
The push starts strong
and fades to nothing.

9
00:00:22,547 --> 00:00:25,047
Which force do we use to find the speed?

10
00:00:25,547 --> 00:00:27,033
None of them alone.

11
00:00:27,033 --> 00:00:29,003
We add up every tiny push.

12
00:00:29,003 --> 00:00:30,117
Let's see how.

13
00:00:30,767 --> 00:00:34,659
For a steady force, the change
in K was simply F times d.

14
00:00:35,009 --> 00:00:39,398
On a force against position
graph, F times d is a rectangle.

15
00:00:39,398 --> 00:00:41,124
Its area is the work.

16
00:00:41,524 --> 00:00:45,402
A changing force makes a slope
or a curve, not a rectangle.

17
00:00:45,802 --> 00:00:47,987
So cut the path into tiny steps.

18
00:00:47,987 --> 00:00:51,058
In each tiny step, the
force is almost steady.

19
00:00:51,708 --> 00:00:52,608
Let's run it.

20
00:00:52,544 --> 00:00:56,166
A two kilogram block, against a
spring squeezed by forty centimetres.

21
00:00:56,515 --> 00:00:59,018
The spring constant is
fifty newtons per metre.

22
00:00:59,018 --> 00:01:01,651
So at the start, it pushes
with twenty newtons.

23
00:01:02,051 --> 00:01:02,951
Release!

24
00:01:02,659 --> 00:01:05,177
The push fades to zero
as the spring opens.

25
00:01:05,177 --> 00:01:06,495
The block shoots off.

26
00:01:06,895 --> 00:01:07,795
Slow-mo replay.

27
00:01:07,761 --> 00:01:10,342
Cut the push into four
steps of ten centimetres.

28
00:01:10,692 --> 00:01:13,493
In each step, the force is almost steady.

29
00:01:13,493 --> 00:01:16,655
So the work is a thin
strip: force times step.

30
00:01:17,055 --> 00:01:21,993
One point seven five, one point two
five, point seven five, point two five

31
00:01:21,993 --> 00:01:22,893
joules.

32
00:01:22,660 --> 00:01:24,791
They shrink as the push fades.

33
00:01:25,191 --> 00:01:26,109
Add them up.

34
00:01:26,109 --> 00:01:27,009
Four joules.

35
00:01:26,974 --> 00:01:29,363
That is the area under the force graph.

36
00:01:29,763 --> 00:01:32,482
The block leaves at two metres per second.

37
00:01:32,482 --> 00:01:35,489
Half times two times two
squared: four joules.

38
00:01:35,489 --> 00:01:37,670
The area equals the change in K.

39
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:40,691
Now the proof, one step at a time.

40
00:01:41,041 --> 00:01:41,941
Step one.

41
00:01:41,892 --> 00:01:43,666
K is half m v squared.

42
00:01:43,666 --> 00:01:47,566
So the rate of change of
K is m v times d v by d t.

43
00:01:48,316 --> 00:01:49,234
Step two.

44
00:01:49,234 --> 00:01:52,155
m times d v by d t is the force F.

45
00:01:52,155 --> 00:01:54,437
So d K by d t equals F v.

46
00:01:55,187 --> 00:01:56,260
Step three.

47
00:01:56,260 --> 00:01:57,973
v is d x by d t.

48
00:01:57,973 --> 00:02:01,306
Cancel the d t, and d K equals F d x.

49
00:02:02,056 --> 00:02:02,956
Step four.

50
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:05,580
Add every tiny piece from start to end.

51
00:02:05,580 --> 00:02:08,538
The integral sign just
means: add them all.

52
00:02:09,288 --> 00:02:13,768
So K final minus K initial
equals the integral of F d x.

53
00:02:13,768 --> 00:02:16,248
The area under the F x graph.

54
00:02:16,998 --> 00:02:18,095
Remember this.

55
00:02:18,095 --> 00:02:22,697
For any force, the change in K equals
the area under the force against

56
00:02:22,697 --> 00:02:23,845
position graph.

57
00:02:24,545 --> 00:02:25,727
Steady or changing?

58
00:02:25,727 --> 00:02:26,652
Side by side.

59
00:02:27,002 --> 00:02:29,688
A steady ten newton push
over forty centimetres.

60
00:02:29,688 --> 00:02:30,588
A rectangle.

61
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:31,748
Area, four joules.

62
00:02:32,148 --> 00:02:36,548
The spring push, twenty newtons down to
zero, over the same forty centimetres.

63
00:02:36,548 --> 00:02:37,448
A triangle.

64
00:02:37,338 --> 00:02:38,830
Area, also four joules.

65
00:02:39,430 --> 00:02:40,419
Now you try.

66
00:02:40,419 --> 00:02:43,376
Which push gives the
block more kinetic energy?

67
00:02:43,376 --> 00:02:44,605
Pause and decide.

68
00:02:47,805 --> 00:02:48,705
The same!

69
00:02:48,569 --> 00:02:50,339
Both areas are four joules.

70
00:02:50,339 --> 00:02:52,734
Only the area counts, not the shape.

71
00:02:53,384 --> 00:02:55,380
What if the force turns around?

72
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:56,563
Follow the links.

73
00:02:56,913 --> 00:02:57,813
One.

74
00:02:57,406 --> 00:02:58,991
Force along the motion.

75
00:02:58,991 --> 00:03:01,030
The strip sits above the axis.

76
00:03:01,030 --> 00:03:02,065
Positive work.

77
00:03:02,465 --> 00:03:03,365
Two.

78
00:03:02,949 --> 00:03:04,601
Force against the motion.

79
00:03:04,601 --> 00:03:06,605
The strip sits below the axis.

80
00:03:06,605 --> 00:03:07,621
Negative work.

81
00:03:08,021 --> 00:03:08,921
Three.

82
00:03:08,619 --> 00:03:12,209
The net area is the area
above, minus the area below.

83
00:03:12,609 --> 00:03:13,509
Four.

84
00:03:13,153 --> 00:03:15,466
That net area is the change in K.

85
00:03:15,466 --> 00:03:18,012
It can be positive, zero or negative.

86
00:03:18,612 --> 00:03:19,512
Your turn.

87
00:03:19,503 --> 00:03:24,054
On an F x graph, ten joules sit
above the axis and fifteen below.

88
00:03:24,054 --> 00:03:26,749
Does the body end up faster or slower?

89
00:03:26,749 --> 00:03:28,010
Pause and think.

90
00:03:31,210 --> 00:03:32,110
Slower!

91
00:03:31,887 --> 00:03:36,815
The net area is minus five joules, so
the body loses five joules of kinetic

92
00:03:36,815 --> 00:03:37,715
energy.

93
00:03:38,142 --> 00:03:39,832
Now, one worked example.

94
00:03:40,182 --> 00:03:42,648
A one kilogram body starts at rest.

95
00:03:42,648 --> 00:03:47,222
A force F equals four x newtons
pushes it from zero to three metres.

96
00:03:47,222 --> 00:03:48,425
Find its speed.

97
00:03:48,925 --> 00:03:52,593
At three metres, the force is
four times three, twelve newtons.

98
00:03:52,593 --> 00:03:54,100
The graph is a triangle.

99
00:03:54,850 --> 00:03:57,527
Area equals half times three times twelve.

100
00:03:57,527 --> 00:03:59,160
Eighteen joules of work.

101
00:03:59,910 --> 00:04:03,516
It started at rest, so its
final K is eighteen joules.

102
00:04:04,266 --> 00:04:07,665
Half times one times v
squared equals eighteen.

103
00:04:07,665 --> 00:04:09,596
v squared is thirty six.

104
00:04:09,596 --> 00:04:11,760
v is six metres per second.

105
00:04:12,410 --> 00:04:13,872
Now, the exam trap.

106
00:04:14,222 --> 00:04:17,592
Many students multiply the
final force by the distance.

107
00:04:17,592 --> 00:04:20,076
Twelve times three, thirty six joules.

108
00:04:20,476 --> 00:04:23,432
But the force was smaller
for most of the path.

109
00:04:23,432 --> 00:04:25,418
Use the area: eighteen joules.

110
00:04:26,068 --> 00:04:27,528
Where is this asked?

111
00:04:27,528 --> 00:04:32,045
With a force given as a formula or
a graph, asking for the work or the

112
00:04:32,045 --> 00:04:32,945
speed.

113
00:04:33,243 --> 00:04:34,458
Exam level now.

114
00:04:34,458 --> 00:04:39,606
A two kilogram block moves along x under
F equals eight minus four x newtons.

115
00:04:39,606 --> 00:04:43,324
At x equals zero, its speed
is one metre per second.

116
00:04:43,324 --> 00:04:45,496
Find its speed at four metres.

117
00:04:45,496 --> 00:04:46,763
Pause and solve.

118
00:04:52,163 --> 00:04:53,586
The answer is B.

119
00:04:53,586 --> 00:04:55,285
One metre per second.

120
00:04:55,785 --> 00:04:59,617
The force is positive up to
two metres, then negative.

121
00:05:00,117 --> 00:05:03,023
Area from zero to two
is plus eight joules.

122
00:05:03,023 --> 00:05:05,622
From two to four, it is minus eight.

123
00:05:06,122 --> 00:05:10,578
Net work is zero, so K is
unchanged: one metre per second.

124
00:05:10,578 --> 00:05:13,567
Three is the top speed, at two metres.

125
00:05:14,167 --> 00:05:15,708
Here is your recap.

126
00:05:15,708 --> 00:05:17,292
Screenshot this card.

127
00:05:20,492 --> 00:05:22,431
Can you answer these without help?

128
00:05:22,831 --> 00:05:23,731
One.

129
00:05:23,196 --> 00:05:24,860
A spring's push fades as it opens.

130
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:26,531
How do we find the work it does?

131
00:05:27,430 --> 00:05:28,330
Two.

132
00:05:27,924 --> 00:05:31,331
What does area below the
axis on an F x graph mean?

133
00:05:32,231 --> 00:05:33,131
Three.

134
00:05:32,772 --> 00:05:35,839
A steady push and a spring
push give the same area.

135
00:05:35,839 --> 00:05:37,115
Which gives more K?

136
00:05:38,215 --> 00:05:42,996
Next time: a stretched catapult and a
raised tank hold energy without moving.

137
00:05:42,996 --> 00:05:44,378
Where is it hiding?

138
00:05:44,378 --> 00:05:45,549
Potential energy.
